# --- S45 C1: performance tuning
concept_understand("S45 · Concept 1 · [THEORY]","Performance Tuning — Why It Matters",
    "Performance tuning = making the database return CORRECT answers FASTER using the same hardware. You tune at three levels — the query, the schema/indexes, and the hardware/config — and you always MEASURE before you change anything.",
    ["Three levels: query (rewrite it), schema/index (add the right index), hardware/config (last resort).",
     "Measure first: profile the slow query with EXPLAIN before touching anything — don't guess.",
     "Most wins are cheap: a missing index often beats an expensive server upgrade.",
     "Tuning changes speed only — it must never change the answer the query returns."],
    None,"Tune query → index → hardware, in that order — and measure before you change.",
    "~7 min. Anchor on the results-portal hook: same hardware, correct answers, just faster. Bust 'just buy a bigger server' early.")
add_table_slide("S45 · Concept 1 · scaffolding","The three levels of tuning — and measure first",
    ["Level","What you change","Example fix","Cost / when"],
    [["Query","Rewrite the SQL itself","Add a WHERE filter; drop SELECT *","Cheapest — try first"],
     ["Index / schema","Add or fix an index; adjust design","Index the citizenship_no column","Cheap — big wins"],
     ["Config","DBMS memory / cache settings","Bigger buffer pool / cache","Medium — needs care"],
     ["Hardware","More RAM / CPU / faster disk","Upgrade the server / add SSD","Expensive — last resort"],
     ["Measure (always)","Nothing yet — you PROFILE","Run EXPLAIN on the slow query","Before every change"]],
    per_page=5,widths=[1.5,2.4,2.6,1.9],fs=11,
    note="Work top-down: a query rewrite or a missing index usually beats a costly hardware upgrade. And measure with EXPLAIN before AND after — 'guess and buy' is the expensive plan.")
concept_apply("S45 · Concept 1 · [THEORY]","Performance Tuning — Why It Matters",
    "Daraz's 'search products' during the Dashain sale rush: the same catalogue that answered in 200 ms suddenly takes 4 s under peak traffic. The fix that works is rarely a bigger server — it is a better query plan and the right index on the searched columns, measured with EXPLAIN before and after.",
    "\"It's slow — just buy a bigger server.\" A missing index or a bad query often costs 10×–100× more than the hardware ever could; a bigger server hides the problem for a while and then it returns. Measure first, tune the query and index, and only then consider hardware.",
    "Performance tuning makes the database return correct answers faster on the same hardware, applied at three levels — query, schema/index, and hardware/config — always measuring (e.g. with EXPLAIN) before changing. Because a missing index or a poor query usually dominates the cost, tuning the query and index typically beats an expensive hardware upgrade.",
    "performance tuning · query/index/hardware levels · measure first · EXPLAIN · index beats hardware")

# --- S45 C2: indexing
concept_understand("S45 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]  (recap Unit 5)","Indexing",
    "An index is an auxiliary structure (usually a B-tree) that lets the DBMS FIND rows without scanning the whole table. It is the single biggest read-speed lever — but it is not free.",
    ["Index the columns used in WHERE, JOIN, and ORDER BY — those are the lookups that hurt.",
     "It speeds up reads/lookups, but slows down INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE (the index must be maintained).",
     "Without an index the DBMS does a full table scan — it reads every row to find a few.",
     "An index also costs storage space, so index deliberately, not everything."],
    "s45_index.png","An index is a book's back-of-book index — without it you read every page.",
    "~7 min. Lean on the book analogy on the slide face. Recap from Unit 5: this is why we index primary/foreign keys.")
add_table_slide("S45 · Concept 2 · examples","When an index HELPS — and when it HURTS",
    ["Situation","Index it?","Why"],
    [["WHERE citizenship_no = ? (KYC lookup)","✅ Yes","Turns a full scan into an instant jump"],
     ["JOIN customer ON account.cust_id","✅ Yes","Foreign-key joins get far faster"],
     ["ORDER BY txn_date on a report","✅ Yes","Avoids sorting the whole table"],
     ["A tiny 50-row lookup table","❌ No","A scan is already instant; index is overhead"],
     ["A column that is written constantly","⚠️ Careful","Every write must update the index too"],
     ["Indexing every column 'to be safe'","❌ No","Slows all writes; wastes space; rarely used"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[3.0,1.2,2.6],fs=11,
    note="Index the columns you FILTER, JOIN, and SORT on — not everything. Each extra index is a tax on every insert/update, so more indexes is not automatically faster.")
concept_apply("S45 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","Indexing",
    "A bank puts an index on the citizenship_no column of its customer table so a KYC lookup — 'find this citizen' — is instant instead of scanning millions of rows. The same is true for eSewa indexing user_id on its transactions table so a user's history opens immediately.",
    "\"More indexes always = faster.\" Every index must be updated on every insert/update/delete, so too many indexes slow down writes, bloat storage, and can even confuse the optimizer. Index the columns you actually filter, join, and sort on — not every column.",
    "An index is an auxiliary (usually B-tree) structure that finds rows without a full table scan, dramatically speeding up reads on the indexed columns (those in WHERE / JOIN / ORDER BY). The trade-off is that indexes slow down writes and consume storage, so they must be chosen deliberately rather than added to every column.",
    "index · B-tree · full table scan · WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY · reads faster, writes slower · index deliberately")

# --- S45 C3: query tuning
concept_understand("S45 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","Query Tuning",
    "Query tuning is rewriting a query so the optimizer can answer it with less work — usually by reading fewer rows. You read the EXECUTION PLAN (EXPLAIN) to see WHY it is slow, then fix the query, not the server.",
    ["Avoid SELECT * — fetch only the columns you need.",
     "Filter early and on an indexed column (e.g. user_id + a date range) so the DB reads few rows.",
     "Read the plan with EXPLAIN: a 'Seq Scan' on a huge table is the red flag.",
     "Avoid unnecessary nested subqueries; prefer joins the optimizer can drive with an index."],
    "s45_queryplan.png","Read the plan (EXPLAIN), filter on an indexed column, stop scanning everything.",
    "~8 min. Walk the before/after on the diagram: Seq Scan 4 s → Index Scan 0.2 s. The fix was the query + index, not hardware.")
add_table_slide("S45 · Concept 3 · §7A depth","Query-tuning techniques → when to use each",
    ["Technique","Problem it fixes","When to use","Nepal / IT example"],
    [["Add / use an index","Full table scan on a filter","WHERE / JOIN on a big table","Index user_id on eSewa txns"],
     ["Filter early (good WHERE)","Reading rows you'll discard","Query touches a huge table","user_id + date range on history"],
     ["Select only needed columns","Hauling unused data","SELECT * on a wide table","Fetch amount, date — not *"],
     ["Rewrite nested subquery as JOIN","Optimizer can't use an index","Correlated subquery is slow","Merchant report joins, not sub-selects"],
     ["Read the EXPLAIN plan","You're guessing at the cause","Any slow query, before tuning","Spot a Seq Scan on 2M rows"],
     ["Paginate / LIMIT results","Returning millions of rows","List/report screens","Show 20 transactions per page"],
     ["Cache a hot result","Same heavy query repeated","Read-mostly, rarely changes","Cache the product catalogue"]],
    per_page=7,widths=[2.2,2.3,2.2,2.6],fs=10.5,
    note="Match the technique to the symptom you see in the EXPLAIN plan. Almost every real win is 'read fewer rows' — via an index, a tighter filter, or fewer columns — not a bigger machine.")
concept_apply("S45 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","Query Tuning",
    "eSewa's transaction-history page is slow because the query scans ALL transactions and then filters. The fix: filter first on an indexed user_id plus a date range, select only the needed columns, and paginate — the EXPLAIN plan flips from a full Seq Scan (seconds) to an Index Scan (milliseconds), on the same hardware.",
    "\"More indexes / a bigger server will fix any slow query.\" Often the query itself is the problem — SELECT * with no useful filter forces a full scan no matter the hardware. Reading the EXPLAIN plan and rewriting the query (filter early, fewer columns, indexable joins) is usually the real fix.",
    "Query tuning rewrites a query so the optimizer reads fewer rows: avoid SELECT *, filter early on indexed columns, read the EXPLAIN execution plan to find the bottleneck (e.g. a full Seq Scan), avoid needless nested subqueries, and prefer index-drivable joins. It targets the query — not the hardware — and is verified by measuring the plan before and after.",
    "query tuning · EXPLAIN / execution plan · avoid SELECT * · filter early · Seq Scan vs Index Scan · joins over subqueries")

# --- S45 C4: authentication vs authorization
concept_understand("S45 · Concept 4 · [THEORY]","Database Security — Authentication vs Authorization",
    "Two different gates protect a database. AUTHENTICATION answers 'WHO are you?' (login, password, OTP). AUTHORIZATION answers 'WHAT are you allowed to do?' (GRANT/REVOKE privileges, roles). You pass authentication first, then authorization decides your access.",
    ["Authentication verifies identity: username + password, OTP, biometrics.",
     "Authorization controls privileges: GRANT SELECT / REVOKE, and roles that bundle privileges.",
     "Order matters: authenticate first (who), then authorize (what) — one without the other is unsafe.",
     "Least privilege: give each user only the access their job needs, nothing more."],
    "s45_security.png","Authentication = ID at the gate; authorization = which rooms your keycard opens.",
    "~7 min. Use the keycard analogy on the face. Contrast a login (auth) with a support agent who can view but not refund (authz).")
add_comparison_table("S45 · Concept 4 · comparison","Authentication vs Authorization",
    ["Dimension","Authentication","Authorization"],
    [["Question it answers","Who are you?","What are you allowed to do?"],
     ["Comes","First","After authentication"],
     ["Mechanism","Login, password, OTP, biometrics","GRANT / REVOKE, roles, privileges"],
     ["Nepal example","Khalti OTP login","Agent can view but not refund"],
     ["If missing","Anyone can log in as anyone","A logged-in user can do anything"],
     ["Analogy","Showing ID at the gate","Which rooms your keycard opens"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[1.8,2.6,2.6],fs=11,
    note="They are two SEPARATE gates, not synonyms. A system can authenticate you correctly and still (wrongly) authorize you to do things you shouldn't — the classic 'logged-in user can delete anything' bug.")
concept_apply("S45 · Concept 4 · [THEORY]","Authentication vs Authorization",
    "Khalti logs you in with an OTP — that is authentication (proving who you are). Once inside, a support agent can VIEW a transaction but cannot issue a refund, while a manager can — that is authorization (what each role may do). Both gates run on every request: identity first, then privilege.",
    "\"Once you're logged in, you can do anything.\" Logging in is only authentication; a separate authorization layer must still restrict each user to their allowed actions (least privilege). Skipping it is how a normal user ends up able to read or delete other people's data.",
    "Authentication verifies WHO a user is (login, password, OTP, biometrics); authorization decides WHAT that authenticated user may do (GRANT/REVOKE, roles, privileges). Authentication always comes first, then authorization applies least privilege. Both are required: identity without privilege control, or vice-versa, leaves the database exposed.",
    "authentication (who) · authorization (what) · GRANT / REVOKE · roles · least privilege · two separate gates")

# --- S45 C5: SQL injection
concept_understand("S45 · Concept 5 · [THEORY]","SQL Injection",
    "SQL injection is an attack where a user types malicious SQL into an input field and the application runs it as part of a real query — letting the attacker read, bypass, or alter the database. Its root cause is building queries by CONCATENATING user input.",
    ["Cause: user text is glued straight into the SQL string, so input becomes code.",
     "Effect: attacker can log in as admin, dump data, or delete tables.",
     "Fix: parameterized queries / prepared statements — input is data, never code.",
     "Defence in depth: also validate input and grant least privilege (Concept 4)."],
    None,"If user input can become SQL code, you've built a door with no lock.",
    "~8 min. Show the ' OR '1'='1 login bypass on the next solved-problem slide, then the parameterized fix. Bust 'hide the errors' as a non-fix.")
add_solved_problem("S45 · Concept 5 · worked","How ' OR '1'='1 logs an attacker in — and the fix",
    "A login form builds its query by pasting the typed username and password straight into the SQL string. An attacker types  ' OR '1'='1  as the password. What happens, and how do we stop it?",
    ["The app builds:  SELECT * FROM users WHERE user='admin' AND pass='' OR '1'='1'",
     "Because '1'='1' is ALWAYS true, the WHERE clause is true for every row — the check is bypassed.",
     "The attacker is logged in (often as the first/admin user) without knowing any password.",
     "Root cause: user input was CONCATENATED into the query, so it became executable code.",
     "Fix — use a parameterized / prepared statement:  WHERE user=? AND pass=?  with values bound separately.",
     "Now the input ' OR '1'='1 is treated as a literal password string, not SQL — the login simply fails."],
    "Never concatenate user input into SQL. Use parameterized queries / prepared statements so input is always DATA, never code — plus input validation and least privilege as backup layers.",
    "~6 min. Type the malicious string live if you can; the 'always true' insight is the whole lesson. Then contrast with the ? placeholder.")
add_examples_table("S45 · Concept 5 · examples","Vulnerable vs safe — the same query two ways",
    ["Pattern","What the code does","Safe?","Why"],
    [["String concatenation","\"...user='\"+u+\"'\"","❌ No","User input becomes executable SQL"],
     ["Parameterized query","WHERE user=? bind(u)","✅ Yes","Input is treated as data, never code"],
     ["Hiding error messages","Suppress DB errors only","❌ No","Obscurity isn't a control; still injectable"],
     ["Input validation","Reject odd characters","⚠️ Helps","A useful extra layer, not a full fix alone"],
     ["Least privilege DB user","App can't DROP tables","⚠️ Helps","Limits the blast radius if injected"],
     ["Stored procedure (parametrized)","Call proc with bound args","✅ Yes","Same principle — data kept out of code"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[2.0,2.3,1.0,2.4],fs=10.5,
    note="The one real fix is keeping user input OUT of the SQL code (parameterized queries / prepared statements). Validation and least privilege reduce risk but never replace it; hiding errors does nothing.")
concept_apply("S45 · Concept 5 · [THEORY]","SQL Injection",
    "A Nepali web app's login form pastes the typed password into its SQL. An attacker enters  ' OR '1'='1  and is logged in as admin — no password needed — because the injected condition is always true. The same flaw lets attackers dump a KYC table through a search box built the same careless way.",
    "\"Hiding the SQL and the error messages makes it secure.\" Obscurity is not a control — the attacker doesn't need to see your errors to inject. The only real fix is parameterized queries / prepared statements (input treated as data), backed by input validation and least privilege.",
    "SQL injection is an attack that inserts malicious SQL through an input field, exploiting queries built by concatenating user input, to bypass logins or read/alter data (e.g. ' OR '1'='1 making a WHERE clause always true). The fix is parameterized queries / prepared statements so input is data not code, reinforced by input validation and least privilege — not hiding error messages.",
    "SQL injection · concatenation is the cause · ' OR '1'='1 · parameterized / prepared statements · input validation · obscurity ≠ security")

# --- S45 C6: encryption + threats
concept_understand("S45 · Concept 6 · [THEORY]","Encryption & the Security Threat Landscape",
    "Encryption encodes data so only parties with the key can read it. Two flavours: AT REST (stored data on disk/backups) and IN TRANSIT (data moving over the network, via TLS/HTTPS). Even a stolen database dump is useless without the keys.",
    ["At rest: encrypt stored account data and backups, so a stolen dump is unreadable.",
     "In transit: TLS/HTTPS protects data travelling between the app and the server.",
     "Encryption is one control among several — pair it with authentication, authorization, and safe queries.",
     "Keys are the crown jewels: lose control of the key and encryption gives you nothing."],
    None,"Encryption = a locked safe; even if the thief takes the safe, they can't open it without the key.",
    "~7 min. Tie the whole session together: the security table lists the threats and the matching control — encryption is the answer to 'stolen data', not to every threat.")
add_examples_table("S45 · Concept 6 · §7A depth","Database security threats → the control that stops it",
    ["Threat","What the attacker does","Control that stops it","Nepal / IT example"],
    [["Unknown user gets in","Logs in without valid identity","Authentication (password/OTP)","Khalti OTP at login"],
     ["User does too much","Acts beyond their role","Authorization / least privilege","Agent can view, not refund"],
     ["SQL injection","Injects SQL via an input box","Parameterized queries + validation","Fix the login form's query"],
     ["Stolen database dump","Reads a copied data file","Encryption at rest","Bank encrypts stored accounts"],
     ["Network eavesdropping","Sniffs data in transit","Encryption in transit (TLS/HTTPS)","HTTPS on mobile banking"],
     ["Data loss / disaster","DB is destroyed or corrupted","Backups + recovery (Unit 6)","Nightly encrypted backups"],
     ["Weak / shared passwords","Guesses or reuses credentials","Strong-password policy + MFA","OTP as a second factor"]],
    per_page=7,widths=[1.9,2.2,2.3,2.4],fs=10.5,
    note="Security is layered — each threat has its own matching control, and you need them together. Encryption answers 'stolen/sniffed data'; it does NOT stop SQL injection or over-broad privileges — those need their own controls.")
concept_apply("S45 · Concept 6 · [THEORY]","Encryption & the Threat Landscape",
    "A Nepali bank encrypts stored account data AT REST and runs HTTPS (TLS) on its mobile-banking app so data IN TRANSIT is protected. If a laptop or backup is stolen, the dump is unreadable without the keys; if traffic is sniffed on public Wi-Fi, it's just ciphertext. Encryption pairs with auth, authorization, and safe queries.",
    "\"Encryption alone makes the database secure.\" Encryption only protects data at rest and in transit — it does nothing against SQL injection, weak passwords, or an over-privileged account. Real security is layered: each threat needs its matching control, and keys must be guarded.",
    "Encryption encodes data so only key-holders can read it, applied at rest (stored data and backups) and in transit (TLS/HTTPS), making a stolen dump or sniffed traffic useless without the keys. It is one layer of a defence-in-depth strategy alongside authentication, authorization/least privilege, and parameterized queries — each threat mapped to its own control.",
    "encryption · at rest vs in transit · TLS/HTTPS · keys · defence in depth · one control among many")

